# Line-ending normalisation.
#
# This repo is edited on Windows (under OneDrive) but builds and runs on Linux.
# Without this file, editing a file rewrites every line as CRLF and git reports
# the whole file as changed: 38 frontend files once showed +4352/-4355 when only
# 12 of them had any real edit. Storing LF in the repo kills that noise.
#
# `text=auto` = git decides what is text, stores LF, and checks out using the
# platform default. It does NOT rewrite history; to clean the existing working
# tree run `git add --renormalize .` as its own separate commit.
* text=auto

# Always LF, regardless of platform: these are read by Linux containers and by
# shells where a stray CR is a syntax error, not cosmetic.
*.py     text eol=lf
*.sh     text eol=lf
*.yml    text eol=lf
*.yaml   text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.sql    text eol=lf

# Always CRLF: Windows-native scripts.
*.bat    text eol=crlf
*.cmd    text eol=crlf

# Never touch these — normalisation would corrupt them.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.webp   binary
*.ico    binary
*.svg    text
*.pdf    binary
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary
*.ttf    binary
*.sqlite3 binary
